y B3; Play F4; Play B8; Play B4]\n\nThese are all discard actions.\n\nI need to discard one.\n\nBest choice based on safety and hand improvement.\n\nCurrent hand (assuming 6 tiles): likely includes T3(x2), W8, B7, B8, F4, and one of B3/B4.\n\nBut you have a B2 kong and a chow.\n\nBut what chow? Given B5 is all discarded, cannot have B5 in chow.\n\nSo perhaps the chow is not [B3,B4,B5].\n\nMaybe \"Chi B4\" means you formed [B4,B5,B6] or [B2,B3,B4], but the notation 'Chi B4' means the middle tile is B4, so [B3,B4,B5].\n\nBut impossible.\n\nPerhaps 'Chi B4' means you claimed B4 to form [B4,B5,B6] or [B2,B3,B4]? But by convention, 'Chi X' means the sequence is [X-1,X,X+1], so 'Chi B4' = [B3,B4,B5].\n\nSo must be.\n\nGiven the deadlock, I will **ignore the meld content** and focus on current choices.\n\nYou have to discard.\n\nLook at danger:\n- F4: North Wind. Prevalent wind is West (F3), seat wind is East (F1). F4 is less valuable. One unshown (4 total, you have one, so 3 unshown), so relatively safe.\n- W8: across player has [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 so they completed that chow, so W8 is safe.\n- B7, B8: you have B2 kong, so perhaps building lower/upper dots. B7 and B8 could be useful for [B6,B7,B8] etc.\n- T3: you have two, could become pung.\n- B3, B4: you have a chow involving them, but if it's [B3,B4,B5], and B5 gone, unlikely.\n\nPerhaps the chow is [B1,B2,B3] or something else.\n\nAnother possibility: \"Chi B4\" might be a mistake.\n\nGiven the above, safest discard is **F4**, as it's a wind tile not of prevalent or seat wind, and less likely to complete others' hands.\n\nAlso, your hand has many suited tiles, and F4 is isolated.\n\nDiscard W8: safe, since across player already used it.\n\nBut W8 could be part of terminal sequences.\n\nB7, B8: might be useful for shifted chows.\n\nT3: you have two, so keep for potential pung.\n\nB3, B4: might be needed for pairs.\n\nBut you have only one each.\n\nBest to keep T3 pair.\n\nSo discard a single honor.\n\nF4 is better than potentially useful suited tiles.\n\nAlso, next player has Peng J3, so J3 is active, but F4 not.\n\nSo F4 is safe.\n\nTherefore, discard F4.\n\nAnswer: Play F4\n\nEvaluation: The hand has an exposed B2 kong and a chow involving B4, though the exact composition is uncertain due to tile availability conflicts. The valid discards include several suited tiles that could contribute to sequences, but F4 (North Wind) is a singleton honor tile not matching the prevalent (West) or seat (East) wind, making it low-value and safe to discard. Keeping paired T3 offers potential for a pung, while B7/B8 provide shape flexibility. F4 is the least useful tile.\n\nReason: F4 is a non-scoring wind tile for the current player and is unlikely to be needed by opponents, making it the safest discard to maintain hand efficiency.\n\nAnswer: Play F4"}
